<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Württemberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1083 CE – 1495 CE (approx.) · capital Stuttgart</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Swabian county that grew from a single hilltop castle into the leading territory of south-west Germany, becoming a duchy in 1495.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-i-of-wurttemberg">Conrad of Wirtemberg</a> is first mentioned in 1081 to 1083, with a castle on the Wirtemberg hill near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-swabia">Duchy of Swabia</a> in 1268, Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-i-count-of-wurttemberg">Ulrich I</a> and his successors bought and seized many of its lands, building a compact territory on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-i-count-of-wurttemberg">Eberhard I</a> fought the Swabian imperial cities and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Henry VII</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> became the residence in the 14th century. The county was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-urach">Urach</a> and Stuttgart lines in 1442. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-i-duke-of-wurttemberg">Count Eberhard V the Bearded</a> reunited it by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-munsingen">Treaty of Münsingen</a> in 1482 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-tubingen">University of Tübingen</a> in 1477.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1495 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms-1495">Diet of Worms</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Maximilian I</a> raised Württemberg to a duchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-i-duke-of-wurttemberg">Eberhard the Bearded</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-wurttemberg">Duchy of Württemberg</a> became an electorate in 1803 and a kingdom in 1806, and today forms part of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="County of Württemberg" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wuerttemberg_Banner.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1083 CE – 1495 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stuttgart</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ulrich I of Württemberg, Eberhard I of Württemberg, Eberhard V the Bearded</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wirtemberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1289&amp;focus=county-of-wurttemberg" aria-label="County of Württemberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4892 474 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Württemberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5212 730L5229 694M5212 730L5212 730M5212 730L5219 736"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5212 730h0M5219 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 730h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1289&amp;focus=county-of-wurttemberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
